	(text "Net tie circumvents\nclearance violation\nfor nodes classified \nas high voltage"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 159.512 112.522 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "862b80a8-18a5-4f2f-90d6-8028077786e0")
	)
	(text "Revision: desolder the transistors\n\nOriginally, I noticed that I had ordered\na wrongly specced part with too much\ncapacitance (MMBFJ113, 28 pF vs MMBF4416,\n4 pF). That would most likely destabilize\nthe feedback loop.\n\nHowever, we don't even need these. The maximum\nplausible voltage is ±15 V, less than ±20 V.\nThe absence of these means the DAC may attempt\nto push SENSEN as far away from ground as\npossible (the transistors limited it to ±0.6 V).\n\nDAC81404 and DAC81401 datasheets say SENSEN\nshould never go more than 12 V above ground. With\nthe SENSEN pin fully disconnected, it always floats\n0.18 V above ground and the respective output is the\nprogrammed voltage above or below 0.18 V.\n\nWith PA95 connected, the behavior changes. Now it's\napproximately halfway between OUTx and GND. And large\nvoltages (past ~±6 V) are clamped to the supply. When\nthe PA95 is powered, the disparity between SENSEN and\nGND vanishes."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 79.756 15.748 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "a919c6e4-5966-43ad-a4cc-aed5f1443551")
	)
